WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.520
- So Amber Heard's attorney
objected to his own question.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:03.540
I gotta see this.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.560
- You were not informed
as to what caused damage

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.656
to Mr. Depp's hand, finger on March 8th?

00:00:08.656 --> 00:00:09.489
- Okay. So that's hearsay.

00:00:09.489 --> 00:00:10.322
- Objection, hearsay.

00:00:10.322 --> 00:00:11.155
- He's objecting to hearsay.

00:00:11.155 --> 00:00:12.330
You're asking what they were told.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:13.163
- Sustain the objection.

00:00:13.163 --> 00:00:13.996
Next question.

00:00:13.996 --> 00:00:15.831
- You didn't know what could cause damage

00:00:15.831 --> 00:00:17.360
to Mr. Depp's hand while
you were there on March 8th.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:18.580
- That's an ambiguous question.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:20.300
You can know something
by being told something

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.210
or just viewing it firsthand.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:25.230
- Dr. Gabriel told me
he sustained an injury

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.300
on one of his fingers.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.642
- Objection. Hearsay.

00:00:27.642 --> 00:00:29.309
- You asked the question.

00:00:29.309 --> 00:00:30.450
- Oh boy.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:32.600
The problem here is that he
asked an ambiguous question

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:33.970
that elicited a hearsay response.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:35.090
I mean, it's complicated.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:36.950
It might not be hearsay,
that's a different issue.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:38.570
And it's not that he
objected to his own question,

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:40.320
it's that he objected to the testimony.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.630
What he should have done is made a motion

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.610
to the court to strike
the answer as hearsay,

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:44.820
but effectively, the judge is saying,

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.630
you asked the question,
it might not be hearsay

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:47.910
so you can live with the answer.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.660
And everyone is exasperated here.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.330
It's not the finest
lawyering I've ever seen

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.540
but being a trial lawyer is hard.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.470
You only have a split
second, and this is a totally

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:55.990
normal thing that happens all the time.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.450
albeit phrased in an
unfortunate way that kind of

00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.800
makes this lawyer look bad.

